{"meta":{"lessonId":"grade_2_math_ch14_seg3","grade":2,"subject":"math","language":"en","topic":"2-D and 3-D Shape Patterns","chapterTitle":"Ch.14 · Pattern Parade: The Rhythm of Numbers","minutes":40,"sloDescriptions":["To continue a shape pattern, find the complete part that repeats, say it in order, and start that same order again."],"source":"v9_html","adapterV2":2},"goal":"To continue a shape pattern, find the complete part that repeats, say it in order, and start that same order again.","sloFull":"To continue a shape pattern, find the complete part that repeats, say it in order, and start that same order again.","sloCode":"M-02-PA-02","hook":{"minutes":4,"warmUp":{"teacherAsks":"Say: 'Dekho aur batao' — Open p.275 and look at the food tray. A samosa is the shape of a ...?","expectedAnswer":"Triangle."},"conversation":[{"teacherAsks":"Look at the food tray on p.275: samosa, tomato, cake. Everyone, together: what shape comes after the circle?"}],"keyWords":[{"term":"2-D shape","def":"Flat shape, like the triangle, circle, square on p.275."},{"term":"3-D shape","def":"Solid shape, like the cone, sphere, cube on p.276."}],"board":{"content":"Pattern: triangle, circle, square, triangle, circle, square"},"materials":["textbook pp.275-276","pencils","student notebooks"]},"iDo":{"minutes":8,"steps":[{"n":1,"action":"Hold up the book open at p.275. Point to the food tray: samosa, tomato, cake, samosa, tomato, cake.","say":"The book says this is a repeating pattern of triangle, circle, square."},{"n":2,"action":"Turn to p.276. Point to the cone, sphere, cube row.","say":"The samosa becomes a cone, the gulab jamun becomes a sphere, the barfi becomes a cube — now it is solid, 3-D."},{"n":3,"say":"The repeating part is cone, sphere, cube. After cube, we start again: cone, sphere."},{"n":4,"say":"A square is flat on paper; a cube you could hold in your hand. Say it with me once, together: 'flat is 2-D, solid is 3-D.'"}],"cfu":"CFU (whole class, thumbs): Point to the cube on p.276 and ask: is this flat or solid? Thumbs up for solid, thumbs down for flat.","misconception":{"fix":"If pupils call the cube a square, say: 'Can you pick it up? Then it is solid, a cube.'"}},"weDo":{"minutes":12,"strategy":"Whole-class game tied to the page, checked together with claps, calling out, and raised books — never one child at a time.","steps":[{"n":1,"action":"Play Pattern Clap: say 'jump, clap, stomp' and have the whole class repeat the actions together twice, seated at their desks."},{"n":2,"action":"Point to p.275 food tray. Whole class calls out the pattern together: triangle, circle, square, triangle, circle, square."},{"n":3,"action":"Point to p.276 Practice Q1a (square pattern row). Whole class calls out what comes next, then everyone draws the next 2 shapes in their notebook."}],"cfu":"Whole-class check: say 'Books up!' — all pupils raise their notebooks open at Q1a at the same time so you can scan for the next 2 shapes drawn."},"youDo":{"minutes":12,"problems":[{"n":1,"prompt":"Textbook p.276 Practice Question 1b: cylinder, sphere, cube, cylinder, sphere, cube. In your notebook, draw the next 2 shapes alone.","answer":"Cylinder, then sphere."},{"n":2,"prompt":"Textbook p.275 Adventure Begins Q3: draw the next two 2D shapes in the food tray pattern alone in your notebook.","answer":"Triangle, then circle."}],"behind":{"prompt":"Say the part that repeats aloud first, point to it twice in the book, then draw only the first next shape alone."},"ahead":{"prompt":"Make a new pattern with the same 3-D shapes in a different order: sphere, cube, cylinder, sphere, cube, cylinder. Draw the next two shapes alone."}},"wrap":{"minutes":4,"exitOptions":[{"prompt":"Say: 'Yeh karke dikhao' — New item on the board: cone, cylinder, cone, cylinder. Draw the next 2 shapes and say the part that repeats. Whole class check: thumbs up or down together.","answer":"Cone, then cylinder. The part that repeats is cone, cylinder."}],"reteach":"2-D shapes are flat; 3-D shapes are solid. Find the part that repeats and start it again.","homework":["At home, line up 6 things in a repeating pattern, such as ball, box, ball, box, ball, box. Draw it in your notebook and write the shape name under each one."]}}
